WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:11.145 --> 00:00:13.903
Now I’d like to introduce
today’s presenters.

00:00:13.903 --> 00:00:16.460
Neil Daswani is an instructor and
co-director for

00:00:16.460 --> 00:00:19.322
the Stanford Advanced Computer
Security Certificate.

00:00:19.322 --> 00:00:24.561
In the past he has served in a variety
of research, development, teaching,

00:00:24.561 --> 00:00:30.138
and managerial roles at Twitter, Dasient,
Google, and Stanford University.

00:00:30.138 --> 00:00:35.357
Neil is also a co-author of “Foundations
of Security – What every Programmer Needs

00:00:35.357 --> 00:00:40.140
to Know”, and has published extensively
in these areas having been quoted by

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:44.575
publications such as the New York Times,
USA Today, and CSO Magazine.

00:00:44.575 --> 00:00:49.113
Neil earned his PhD and MS degrees
in computer science at Stanford, and

00:00:49.113 --> 00:00:52.981
he holds a BS in computer science
with honors from Columbia.

00:00:52.981 --> 00:00:55.804
Now I’d like to turn
the floor over to Neil.

00:00:55.804 --> 00:00:58.070
Great, thank you very much for
that introduction Joe.

00:00:58.070 --> 00:01:03.075
In addition to the company that
you mentioned, I've also served

00:01:03.075 --> 00:01:08.808
in managerial roles In the area of
security both at LifeLock and Symantec,

00:01:08.808 --> 00:01:13.813
and I'm thrilled to be chatting
with all of you today about some of

00:01:13.813 --> 00:01:19.000
the big name breaches that have
taken place over the past few years.

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:24.060
If we look at what's gone on in
the field for the past 15 years,

00:01:24.060 --> 00:01:30.090
there have been more than a billion
customer records that have been stolen.

00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:33.888
There have been thousands
upon thousand of breeches

00:01:33.888 --> 00:01:37.943
that have impacted many organizations,
and so

00:01:37.943 --> 00:01:42.820
there's a lot to be learned
from these breeches.

00:01:42.820 --> 00:01:47.330
One of the reasons that I think it's
important to make sure we cover these

00:01:47.330 --> 00:01:53.580
kinds of lessons and that we can all learn
from these lessons is because there's this

00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:59.630
adage that those who don't know history
are doomed to repeat it and I think

00:01:59.630 --> 00:02:04.680
even when you know all the lessons,
We'll look at various world leaders.

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:06.930
They're very aware of history, and still,

00:02:06.930 --> 00:02:12.220
it can be challenging to keep
one's self out of trouble.

00:02:12.220 --> 00:02:17.080
Given what's taken place in the industry,
there's a lot to be learned.

00:02:17.080 --> 00:02:23.430
There's lot of organizations that have
been victimized by significant breaches.

00:02:23.430 --> 00:02:29.562
And the ones that we highlight here
are not ones that we're talking about

00:02:29.562 --> 00:02:34.590
to shame any of them, but rather,
I think, as a community of enterprises,

00:02:34.590 --> 00:02:38.880
we need to all get better and

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:42.810
be able to be on a path of continual
improvement with regards to security, so

00:02:42.810 --> 00:02:47.920
that we can combat some of
the adversaries, whether they

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:53.080
be organized separate criminal groups,
or potentially even nation-states.

00:02:53.080 --> 00:02:57.754
So let's go ahead and
start by covering a few of

00:02:57.754 --> 00:03:03.240
the breaches that have just occurred
in the first five months of this year.

00:03:03.240 --> 00:03:08.481
And I'm going to cover not only
two breaches but one incident

00:03:08.481 --> 00:03:13.190
that may not be considered a breach and
I'll talk a little bit about why that is.

00:03:13.190 --> 00:03:17.360
But if we just look at the first five
months of 2017, there have been over 243

00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:22.830
breaches per data from the breach
database at privacyrights.org.

00:03:22.830 --> 00:03:28.090
And overall, the MO, or the modus
operandi, for some of these breaches

00:03:28.090 --> 00:03:33.050
has been similar to what they have been,
I'd say over the past four to five years.

00:03:33.050 --> 00:03:37.357
The slide around here on the left
talks about the attacker life cycle,

00:03:37.357 --> 00:03:39.150
and I'll cover that in a bit more detail.

00:03:39.150 --> 00:03:43.740
Let me chat a little bit about
some of the more recent breaches.

00:03:43.740 --> 00:03:49.739
One of the organizations that was impacted
by a breach earlier this year is Dun and

00:03:49.739 --> 00:03:50.850
Bradstreet.

00:03:50.850 --> 00:03:53.300
Some of you may have heard of Dun and
Bradstreet.

00:03:53.300 --> 00:03:58.480
They are an organization that tracks
information about businesses mostly for

00:03:58.480 --> 00:04:03.110
the purpose of helping other organizations
assess the credit worthiness

00:04:04.380 --> 00:04:05.970
of those businesses.

00:04:05.970 --> 00:04:11.240
They also happen to have databases
of large numbers of individuals

00:04:11.240 --> 00:04:15.760
in corporate America,
from CEOs to Executive Vice Presidents,

00:04:15.760 --> 00:04:22.080
Senior Vice Presidents to managers
across their organization, and

00:04:22.080 --> 00:04:27.923
one of the issues that occurred earlier
this year in March is that it appears that

00:04:27.923 --> 00:04:33.340
their database of individuals
in corporate America was stolen.

00:04:33.340 --> 00:04:37.748
Now Dun and Bradstreet as a company
sells that database to a number of other

00:04:37.748 --> 00:04:42.310
organizations and from statements
that Dun and Bradstreet have made,

00:04:42.310 --> 00:04:47.020
they don't believe that it was their
information security countermeasures that

00:04:47.020 --> 00:04:52.020
were breached, but they sell this data to
a whole bunch of other organizations and

00:04:52.020 --> 00:04:55.450
they believe that it was actually
one of the other organizations,

00:04:55.450 --> 00:04:58.040
one of the customers that were breached.

00:04:58.040 --> 00:05:01.100
And so one of the things that we
will see in some of the other big

00:05:01.100 --> 00:05:04.767
name breaches that I'm going to talk
about a little bit later is that vetting

00:05:04.767 --> 00:05:08.830
your third-party suppliers has
become more and more important.

00:05:08.830 --> 00:05:12.985
We'll talk about that in relation to,
for instance, the Target breach in a few

00:05:12.985 --> 00:05:17.274
minutes, but what's interesting about
the Dun and Bradstreet breach is that it

00:05:17.274 --> 00:05:21.120
may have been the security of one of
their customers that was breached.

00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:23.770
And so typically when you buy
something from a company,

00:05:23.770 --> 00:05:26.320
you think about okay you need to vet
their information security practices.

00:05:26.320 --> 00:05:30.013
You typically don't think about it
the other way around where somebody buys

00:05:30.013 --> 00:05:32.530
something from you, you have to worry
about their information security

00:05:32.530 --> 00:05:35.530
practices, but
this is an example of where.

00:05:35.530 --> 00:05:40.800
If you're selling data about your
customers or other individuals

00:05:40.800 --> 00:05:44.700
to other companies, well, maybe one
might need to look the other way around.

00:05:45.870 --> 00:05:47.980
That was the Dun and Bradstreet breach.

00:05:47.980 --> 00:05:51.720
Let me chat a little bit next
about the River City Media breach.

00:05:51.720 --> 00:05:56.100
So River City Media is
an email marketing company.

00:05:57.270 --> 00:06:04.520
And earlier this year, they had a list
of about 1.3 billion names and email

00:06:05.560 --> 00:06:11.960
addresses that they would
typically market to.

00:06:11.960 --> 00:06:17.353
And that data effectively was
a breach that effectively

00:06:17.353 --> 00:06:24.466
inadvertently leaked out onto the Internet
because they were using a Unix

00:06:24.466 --> 00:06:30.677
tool called RSYNC to back up their
database of email addresses.

00:06:30.677 --> 00:06:36.116
There was a configuration problem with
that RSIC process, where it was set up so

00:06:36.116 --> 00:06:42.140
that inadvertently allowed the information
to be viewable by anybody on the Internet.

00:06:43.290 --> 00:06:47.290
So that was a second breach
that occurred this year.

00:06:47.290 --> 00:06:52.140
The third issue that I want to talk about,
incident, that I want about for

00:06:52.140 --> 00:06:56.940
this year is the WannaCry Incidents.

00:06:56.940 --> 00:07:01.916
So WannaCry is the short form
of a name of a piece of malware,

00:07:01.916 --> 00:07:08.189
malicious software, that would,
once it infects somebody's machine,

00:07:08.189 --> 00:07:12.569
it would basically encrypt
files on the hard drive or

00:07:12.569 --> 00:07:17.560
the local storage of
the machine that was infected.

00:07:17.560 --> 00:07:19.230
It's a piece of ransomware.

00:07:19.230 --> 00:07:22.930
So once that data got encrypted,
using that's only known to the attacker,

00:07:22.930 --> 00:07:29.150
the attacker would then ask the individual

00:07:29.150 --> 00:07:32.770
company that was affected to pay a ransom,
I believe it was about $300,

00:07:32.770 --> 00:07:38.170
it was a few hundred dollars, if they
want to have their files decrypted.

00:07:38.170 --> 00:07:43.880
So the WannaCry incident had affected over
200,000 machines across the internet,

00:07:43.880 --> 00:07:49.195
and the reason that I say it
wasn't say an incident as opposed

00:07:49.195 --> 00:07:55.329
to a breach is that typically in a breech,
there's an exfiltration

00:07:55.329 --> 00:07:59.930
of the data in an unencrypted way.

00:07:59.930 --> 00:08:04.800
In this case when WannaCry or another
piece of ransomware infects the machine,

00:08:04.800 --> 00:08:09.380
it encrypts the data and keeps it
local to the machine that's infected.

00:08:09.380 --> 00:08:14.248
And it doesn't necessarily need to
get infiltrated, and so that may or

00:08:14.248 --> 00:08:15.980
may not qualify as a breach.

00:08:15.980 --> 00:08:20.876
It certainly qualifies as a compromise,
but if you look at the various

00:08:20.876 --> 00:08:25.565
state breach laws, and, I'll just mention
up front that I'm not an attorney,

00:08:25.565 --> 00:08:28.200
it may or
may not qualify [LAUGH] as a breach.

00:08:28.200 --> 00:08:30.330
But it's certainly
an interesting incident.

00:08:30.330 --> 00:08:34.420
The reason that the WannaCry ransomware
was able to propagate and affect so

00:08:34.420 --> 00:08:39.422
many machines on the internet
was because it took

00:08:39.422 --> 00:08:45.393
advantage of a vulnerability in
a distributed file protocol,

00:08:45.393 --> 00:08:48.150
the SMB protocol on Windows.

00:08:48.150 --> 00:08:54.130
And what was interesting about this is
that the vulnerability that was used,

00:08:54.130 --> 00:08:57.902
there was a patch made
available in mid-March.

00:08:57.902 --> 00:09:02.021
And so if one simply patched
before it hit in May,

00:09:02.021 --> 00:09:05.852
then one's machine should not be affected.

00:09:05.852 --> 00:09:10.900
But it just emphasizes how
important regular patching is.

00:09:10.900 --> 00:09:17.200
So those are three breaches slash
incidents that occurred just this year.

00:09:17.200 --> 00:09:21.708
Now, if you look at some of these numbers,
if you look at the River City Media

00:09:21.708 --> 00:09:25.525
breach, it has a very significant number,
1.34 billion.

00:09:25.525 --> 00:09:28.600
It was mostly names and email addresses.

00:09:28.600 --> 00:09:32.830
There have been larger breaches that have
been that have taken place over the years.

00:09:32.830 --> 00:09:37.086
And more significant breaches in the way
of the information that was stolen.

00:09:37.086 --> 00:09:39.991
So let me go ahead and chat a little bit
about some of those breaches as well.

00:09:41.526 --> 00:09:45.228
Before I go on to talking
about specific breaches,

00:09:45.228 --> 00:09:49.468
we'll start with the autumn
breach in just a second.

00:09:49.468 --> 00:09:54.297
I just want to double click into
the diagram that I had on the previous

00:09:54.297 --> 00:09:55.790
slide a little bit more.

00:09:55.790 --> 00:10:00.574
This diagram shows the typical
attacker lifecycle.

00:10:00.574 --> 00:10:04.252
So very often we can end
up focusing on our work and

00:10:04.252 --> 00:10:08.940
what's convenient and
what we need to do to get our jobs done.

00:10:08.940 --> 00:10:12.750
But attackers whether
they're cyber criminals,

00:10:12.750 --> 00:10:16.320
whether they're nation state attackers,
they have a different view of the world.

00:10:16.320 --> 00:10:20.382
And there's been a number of these types
of diagrams that have been produced over

00:10:20.382 --> 00:10:21.650
the past few years.

00:10:21.650 --> 00:10:26.500
This particular one is
available on Wikipedia.

00:10:26.500 --> 00:10:31.192
But it shows that there is a cycle that

00:10:31.192 --> 00:10:35.400
attackers follow where they first
define what their target is.

00:10:35.400 --> 00:10:39.490
And then they effectively take
a mission based approach to going

00:10:39.490 --> 00:10:41.660
about achieving their goals.

00:10:41.660 --> 00:10:44.490
This is very different than way that
attackers used to operate 10 or

00:10:44.490 --> 00:10:45.290
15 years ago.

00:10:45.290 --> 00:10:49.540
Where very often somebody would write
a worm or a virus and deploy it out into

00:10:49.540 --> 00:10:52.950
the internet and their goal was
just to make a name for themselves.

00:10:52.950 --> 00:10:57.931
That's typically not the case anymore, the
attackers even when they make an initial

00:10:57.931 --> 00:11:00.260
compromise they don't
disclose it to the world.

00:11:00.260 --> 00:11:04.410
Even if they can deface your home page,
they don't do that right away.

00:11:04.410 --> 00:11:09.035
They will look at the initial compromise
as a first step in their attack and

00:11:09.035 --> 00:11:11.734
they'll figure out what else they can do.

00:11:11.734 --> 00:11:15.669
And so once they've defined their target
they will figure out hey are there

00:11:15.669 --> 00:11:19.180
other cybercriminal groups that
they want to work together with?

00:11:19.180 --> 00:11:23.820
They may build or acquire tools to
target particular organizations.

00:11:23.820 --> 00:11:26.100
They may develop custom malware.

00:11:26.100 --> 00:11:30.790
They may understand a little bit
about how an organization works and

00:11:30.790 --> 00:11:33.620
how to target their
particular countermeasures.

00:11:33.620 --> 00:11:38.370
They will research their targets, whether
their targets be executives of a company,

00:11:38.370 --> 00:11:41.520
whether their targets are system
administrators at a company.

00:11:41.520 --> 00:11:44.390
They'll spend time on social networks

00:11:44.390 --> 00:11:48.460
to understand who are the people they
should target, what are their roles?

00:11:48.460 --> 00:11:52.200
If there's an important executive and
maybe they think the executive might not

00:11:52.200 --> 00:11:55.570
fall for the attack,
who is their administrative assistant?

00:11:55.570 --> 00:11:56.679
How can they

00:11:58.050 --> 00:12:03.090
dupe the administrative assistant into
clicking on the wrong thing, for instance?

00:12:03.090 --> 00:12:06.830
Once they develop their tools, they'll
test for detection, they'll deploy,

00:12:06.830 --> 00:12:10.300
they'll make some initial intrusions,
they'll infect some machines.

00:12:10.300 --> 00:12:14.260
They'll get credentials to
some particular account and

00:12:14.260 --> 00:12:16.520
then they will continue their attack.

00:12:16.520 --> 00:12:19.914
Once they establish a footprint
of a compromised machine, say,

00:12:19.914 --> 00:12:24.237
they will initiate an up-down connection
that they can persistently control that

00:12:24.237 --> 00:12:25.796
machine on an ongoing basis.

00:12:25.796 --> 00:12:30.168
And use it as a beachhead to do
additional reconnaissance and

00:12:30.168 --> 00:12:33.960
scanning to see what more
machines can they infect?

00:12:33.960 --> 00:12:36.040
What other information
can they get access to?

00:12:36.040 --> 00:12:39.660
And they'll pretty much continue
this process over months or

00:12:39.660 --> 00:12:41.560
years until they accomplish their mission.

00:12:41.560 --> 00:12:46.050
Whether their mission is to delve into
intellectual property, steal identities,

00:12:46.050 --> 00:12:50.230
steal trade secrets,
and so on and so forth.

00:12:50.230 --> 00:12:54.590
One of the things that this
diagram shows in the center,

00:12:54.590 --> 00:12:56.260
is that not all attackers are equal.

00:12:56.260 --> 00:13:01.310
There are some very determined attackers
that will put in that kind of effort and

00:13:01.310 --> 00:13:05.080
they're typically referred to
as advanced persistent threats.

00:13:05.080 --> 00:13:10.579
And so you'll see as denoted by the purple
that attackers that are in that category

00:13:10.579 --> 00:13:15.774
will carry out all of the steps in this
particular attack lifecycle diagram.

00:13:15.774 --> 00:13:21.957
At the same time there might be, say,
lower tier cybercriminal groups.

00:13:21.957 --> 00:13:27.577
I wouldn't call them lower tier in their
methods of attack, but I call them lower

00:13:27.577 --> 00:13:33.380
tier in, say, the amount of resources that
they have is compared to nation state.

00:13:34.600 --> 00:13:38.610
And they may, in order to accomplish
their goals and their mission,

00:13:38.610 --> 00:13:42.860
if they simply want to monetize
a bunch of machines that they infect.

00:13:42.860 --> 00:13:47.113
And have them participate in a bot net and
rent out those compromised machines

00:13:47.113 --> 00:13:51.990
to others, they may not need to pursue all
of the steps in this particular diagram.

00:13:51.990 --> 00:13:56.170
So if you look at the orange for instance,
you'll see that they may build a required

00:13:56.170 --> 00:13:58.875
tool they may test for
detection, they'll deploy.

00:13:58.875 --> 00:14:03.617
But they may not spend as much time
researching via social media all of

00:14:03.617 --> 00:14:08.791
the employees or individuals in
an organization that they want to target.

00:14:08.791 --> 00:14:12.114
So this is the typical
attacker lifecycle and

00:14:12.114 --> 00:14:17.361
as I talk about some of these large
breaches, you'll see they describe

00:14:17.361 --> 00:14:22.183
some of the different parts of
this in talking about the attacks.

00:14:22.183 --> 00:14:27.942
So, with that, let's talk about
the first breach or set of breaches.

00:14:27.942 --> 00:14:32.522
And we're going to chat about the breaches
that occurred against Yahoo that was

00:14:32.522 --> 00:14:34.083
discovered in 2016.

00:14:34.083 --> 00:14:36.963
And so there's an asterisk
up to the top right of 2016,

00:14:36.963 --> 00:14:39.775
because that's when
the attack was discovered.

00:14:39.775 --> 00:14:42.675
The breaches actually took
place in 2013 and 2014,

00:14:42.675 --> 00:14:45.385
there were actually two distinct breaches.

00:14:45.385 --> 00:14:49.405
They disclosed one breach of over
500 million user records, and

00:14:49.405 --> 00:14:53.350
then another follow on breach
of a billion plus user records.

00:14:56.180 --> 00:15:00.863
And so just for convenience,
I will talk about those

00:15:00.863 --> 00:15:06.220
breaches together as one, even though
they were two distinct breaches.

00:15:07.490 --> 00:15:11.800
If we look at what information
was stolen in those user records,

00:15:11.800 --> 00:15:14.700
it included not just names and
email addresses.

00:15:14.700 --> 00:15:19.120
But it included telephone numbers,
it included the security questions and

00:15:19.120 --> 00:15:22.570
answers that one typically sets up
when you set up a web based account.

00:15:22.570 --> 00:15:26.736
Such that even if somebody didn't have
your password they could try to use

00:15:26.736 --> 00:15:29.860
the answers to those questions
to gain access to your account.

00:15:29.860 --> 00:15:37.126
The stolen information included dates
of birth as well as hacked passwords.

00:15:37.126 --> 00:15:41.240
That said, due to the scope
of the breach that occurred,

00:15:41.240 --> 00:15:43.811
what the attackers were able to do,

00:15:43.811 --> 00:15:49.250
is get access to actually all
the billing across the Yahoo accounts.

00:15:49.250 --> 00:15:53.200
And they were able to log into
any of those accounts at will,

00:15:53.200 --> 00:15:54.850
without a password.

00:15:54.850 --> 00:15:55.690
How did they do that?

00:15:55.690 --> 00:15:57.905
I'll talk about that in just a second.

00:15:57.905 --> 00:16:05.240
So this breach was the largest breach that
occurred in the history of the Internet.

00:16:05.240 --> 00:16:08.681
Yahoo was in the midst
of getting acquired by

00:16:08.681 --> 00:16:13.530
Verizon Communications at that time
that the breach was discovered.

00:16:13.530 --> 00:16:20.221
And it was a breach that was material
enough, they were getting acquired,

00:16:20.221 --> 00:16:25.125
I think, for $4 billion plus,
probably close to $5 billion.

00:16:25.125 --> 00:16:30.687
And upon disclosure of the breach,
given that it was material,

00:16:30.687 --> 00:16:36.561
Verizon dropped the price of
the acquisition by $350 million.

00:16:36.561 --> 00:16:39.657
Which is pretty expensive in and
of itself,

00:16:39.657 --> 00:16:45.099
not including all the costs that Yahoo
probably had to incur to do forensics,

00:16:45.099 --> 00:16:49.700
and investigations, and
paying attorney's fees and whatnot.

00:16:51.760 --> 00:16:53.560
It's a very significant breach,

00:16:53.560 --> 00:16:56.383
probably the most significant
breach of all time, by far.

00:16:56.383 --> 00:16:59.329
It resulted in

00:17:03.297 --> 00:17:07.330
Issues to Yahoo's valuation.

00:17:07.330 --> 00:17:09.490
Those were some of the impacts.

00:17:09.490 --> 00:17:12.548
There are a number of
other impacts as well.

00:17:12.548 --> 00:17:17.240
Given that attackers, and by the way,
the hacks that were conducted attacks

00:17:17.240 --> 00:17:21.918
were made up of two Russian spies,
and two cyber criminals.

00:17:21.918 --> 00:17:26.535
And one of the interesting
documents that I read,

00:17:26.535 --> 00:17:32.205
of course in preparation for this webinar,
was the indictment of those individuals.

00:17:32.205 --> 00:17:35.085
There's a reference to it
at the end of the talk, but

00:17:35.085 --> 00:17:40.533
the attack was significant not only for
these financial reasons.

00:17:40.533 --> 00:17:45.200
But the attackers given that they could

00:17:45.200 --> 00:17:49.460
login to pretty much anybody's Yahoo
account, they started targeting diplomats,

00:17:49.460 --> 00:17:52.180
they started targeted
particular executives.

00:17:52.180 --> 00:17:57.954
They just went after about
6,500 very targeted folks.

00:17:57.954 --> 00:18:03.834
Given, of course, that the list of
all of these records were stolen and

00:18:03.834 --> 00:18:08.153
one could use them to do
math stop phishing attacks.

00:18:08.153 --> 00:18:14.330
But there's many other things
that could've taken place.

00:18:14.330 --> 00:18:19.370
The attackers used a combination of
spear phishing and malware to get in.

00:18:19.370 --> 00:18:24.260
In fact, their attack was significant
enough that they were able to affect Yahoo

00:18:24.260 --> 00:18:28.160
search engines so they could return
whatever links that they wanted at will.

00:18:28.160 --> 00:18:32.760
And so in addition to the attacks that
they conducted, they setup a bunch of

00:18:32.760 --> 00:18:37.583
affiliate deals and started serving
links on Yahoo search engine,

00:18:37.583 --> 00:18:41.829
and monetizing the traffic from
people clicking onto those links.

00:18:43.310 --> 00:18:47.350
So that's a pretty significant impact,
how did that occur?

00:18:47.350 --> 00:18:51.890
So the FBI pretty much traced

00:18:51.890 --> 00:18:56.730
the beginning of this attack
to a phishing attack.

00:18:56.730 --> 00:18:58.890
In particular to a spear phishing attack.

00:18:58.890 --> 00:19:00.960
So those of you who
are familiar with phishing.

00:19:00.960 --> 00:19:05.656
You know that even going back ten years,
there would be

00:19:05.656 --> 00:19:09.430
many attackers that would send out emails
that is claimed to be from some bank.

00:19:09.430 --> 00:19:12.840
And they would hope that when
they sent you that email,

00:19:12.840 --> 00:19:15.580
that you have an account at that bank and
that you'll click on the link and

00:19:15.580 --> 00:19:18.788
you'll go to the imposter web
page that they've set up.

00:19:18.788 --> 00:19:24.860
And enter your username and password for
your bank and the attacker's site

00:19:24.860 --> 00:19:28.300
will just return some errors saying your
login didn't work please try again later.

00:19:28.300 --> 00:19:31.029
But what the attacker will do with
those stolen credentials then

00:19:31.029 --> 00:19:34.110
is go log into your bank and
try to transfer money out of it.

00:19:34.110 --> 00:19:38.480
And those were kind of the initial
state of phishing attacks.

00:19:38.480 --> 00:19:42.220
But over time attackers developed more
sophistication so that they started doing

00:19:42.220 --> 00:19:47.180
spear phishing attacks where the attacks
were not necessarily targeted at

00:19:47.180 --> 00:19:50.900
consumers broadly but they were targeted
at individuals and corporations.

00:19:50.900 --> 00:19:55.665
And the goal was to get particular
individuals to give up their

00:19:55.665 --> 00:20:01.330
credentials for certain corporate
accounts for corporate systems.

00:20:01.330 --> 00:20:05.290
So, why bother trying to look for
vulnerabilities and

00:20:05.290 --> 00:20:10.220
try to break in a whole bunch of machines
if you can just email an employee

00:20:11.580 --> 00:20:15.220
and get them to give
you their credentials.

00:20:15.220 --> 00:20:20.000
And so the attack against Yahoo in
fact started with spear phishing

00:20:20.000 --> 00:20:24.870
against not an executive and
not random employees, but

00:20:24.870 --> 00:20:29.940
a particular employee and set of
employees that were semi privileged,

00:20:29.940 --> 00:20:35.280
that happened to have access
to certain systems at Yahoo.

00:20:35.280 --> 00:20:39.610
Some of the key systems that
mattered were Yahoo's User Database,

00:20:41.220 --> 00:20:46.780
and Yahoo's tool that they used for
account management.

00:20:46.780 --> 00:20:51.630
The results though, another tool
that was used to generate cookies.

00:20:51.630 --> 00:20:55.320
And so let me just finish talking a little
bit about spear phishing and malware.

00:20:55.320 --> 00:20:59.880
I know I've talked a little bit about
how the cookie theft occurred and

00:20:59.880 --> 00:21:03.130
how that allowed hackers to
access all Yahoo email accounts.

00:21:04.870 --> 00:21:10.010
So spear phishing attacks, they very often

00:21:10.010 --> 00:21:14.750
are seeking to steal a set of user
credentials, and employee credentials.

00:21:14.750 --> 00:21:19.720
But in addition given that drive by
downloads can occur where to infect

00:21:19.720 --> 00:21:24.060
a machine these days, you don't even
need to dupe a user into clicking yes,

00:21:24.060 --> 00:21:27.210
or downloading something and
double clicking on it to run.

00:21:27.210 --> 00:21:32.068
You can simply, if you can send over
an email where you can dupe somebody

00:21:32.068 --> 00:21:33.800
into just clicking on a link.

00:21:33.800 --> 00:21:37.680
Then upon the victim clicking on a link,

00:21:37.680 --> 00:21:41.170
they will get infected just
by opening the web page.

00:21:41.170 --> 00:21:46.027
And the way that happens is by drive
by download where the infected web

00:21:46.027 --> 00:21:50.228
page will fingerprint what
browser is the victim running.

00:21:50.228 --> 00:21:53.598
What are all the different third party
plugins that the victim is running.

00:21:53.598 --> 00:21:58.516
And within a couple hundred milliseconds
the attacker's exploit tool kit on

00:21:58.516 --> 00:22:03.054
the infected web page figures out
what vulnerable versions of software

00:22:03.054 --> 00:22:03.975
are running.

00:22:03.975 --> 00:22:07.855
And simply does a lookup in an online
database to figure out what's the piece

00:22:07.855 --> 00:22:11.809
of malicious code that they need to
spin down to exploit that vulnerability,

00:22:11.809 --> 00:22:14.420
infect the machine, and take it over.

00:22:14.420 --> 00:22:19.607
And so spear phishing attacks often also
contain these links to drive by downloads.

00:22:19.607 --> 00:22:23.642
So the attacks against Yahoo
started with spear phishing and

00:22:23.642 --> 00:22:27.130
malware that was used to infect machines.

00:22:27.130 --> 00:22:31.450
And the attacker was able to get
access to these Yahoo systems.

00:22:31.450 --> 00:22:36.190
I mentioned that one of the systems
was a tool that allows the attacker to

00:22:36.190 --> 00:22:37.960
effectively mint cookies.

00:22:37.960 --> 00:22:42.300
So for those of you that
know how both websites work,

00:22:42.300 --> 00:22:45.190
right if there's a website
which requires a login.

00:22:45.190 --> 00:22:48.886
You present the user with a username and
password field and

00:22:48.886 --> 00:22:51.610
have them type in the username and
password.

00:22:51.610 --> 00:22:56.585
And then once their username and password
checks out, you give them back a cookie,

00:22:56.585 --> 00:23:00.159
and a server will give back
a cookie to the user's browser.

00:23:00.159 --> 00:23:04.034
And after that interaction it's that
cookie that keeps going back and

00:23:04.034 --> 00:23:06.858
forth between the website and
the users browser and

00:23:06.858 --> 00:23:09.502
that cookie actually
authenticates the user.

00:23:09.502 --> 00:23:12.170
So that the user doesn't have
to enter their username and

00:23:12.170 --> 00:23:13.850
password on every single web page.

00:23:13.850 --> 00:23:18.780
And so, well, if you're an attacker and
you can figure out how a website creates

00:23:18.780 --> 00:23:23.809
its cookies, then you actually don't
need anybody's password to login,

00:23:23.809 --> 00:23:25.580
you can just forge cookies.

00:23:25.580 --> 00:23:27.770
And login to the website and
go from there.

00:23:27.770 --> 00:23:30.780
And that's exactly what
happened in the Yahoo attack.

00:23:30.780 --> 00:23:36.310
And that's how the attackers were able to
access all Yahoo email accounts at will,

00:23:36.310 --> 00:23:37.110
without passwords.

00:23:37.110 --> 00:23:38.789
So that's the Yahoo breach.

00:23:40.000 --> 00:23:41.830
And that was discovered in 2016.

00:23:41.830 --> 00:23:45.140
I'm going to talk about a couple of other
breaches that were discovered in 2015 and

00:23:45.140 --> 00:23:48.385
2014, and then I'm going to chat
a little bit about some defenses

00:23:48.385 --> 00:23:49.730
against malware and phishing.

00:23:49.730 --> 00:23:53.740
But one thing that you'll see is that
in a lot of these breaches phishing and

00:23:53.740 --> 00:23:57.720
malware have been the tools of choice
that attackers have been using.

00:23:57.720 --> 00:24:01.710
So let me chat about the next breach,

00:24:01.710 --> 00:24:03.950
the Office of Personnel Management
breach of 2015.

00:24:03.950 --> 00:24:11.309
The Office of Personnel Management
is a government agency that has or

00:24:11.309 --> 00:24:16.120
had and still has,
all the background check records.

00:24:16.120 --> 00:24:19.780
So anytime you applied to be a government
employee and or have to go through

00:24:19.780 --> 00:24:24.660
a security clearance, there is a form
called SF86, standard form 86.

00:24:24.660 --> 00:24:29.510
And it not only contains, as you might
imagine, all the previous addresses that

00:24:29.510 --> 00:24:33.510
you used to live and all the places
that you used to work and what not.

00:24:33.510 --> 00:24:36.370
But it has that information for
all of your family members.

00:24:37.620 --> 00:24:42.539
And what got stolen during the Office
of Personnel Management breach

00:24:42.539 --> 00:24:45.220
in 2015 was all of those records.

00:24:45.220 --> 00:24:48.770
It also included, by the way,
5.6 million finger print records.

00:24:49.770 --> 00:24:51.010
And so.

00:24:51.010 --> 00:24:52.890
What with the impact of that attack.

00:24:52.890 --> 00:24:57.734
If you read these health
oversight committees

00:24:57.734 --> 00:25:03.455
majority staff report on this breach,
a direct quote from there,

00:25:03.455 --> 00:25:08.528
in terms of what the impact was is that it
will harm counter intelligence efforts for

00:25:08.528 --> 00:25:10.150
at least a generation to come.

00:25:10.150 --> 00:25:12.300
The value of the stolen
background information for

00:25:12.300 --> 00:25:16.470
a foreign nation cannot be overstated,
nor will it ever be fully known.

00:25:17.850 --> 00:25:22.870
That's quite significant,
the damage has already been done.

00:25:22.870 --> 00:25:27.760
It's a significant blow to
a country's intelligence efforts, and

00:25:27.760 --> 00:25:29.890
counterintelligence efforts.

00:25:29.890 --> 00:25:30.930
How did it happen?

00:25:31.940 --> 00:25:37.072
Overall, the information security program
at the Office of Personnel Management was

00:25:37.072 --> 00:25:44.430
under resourced and the amount of priority
that was given to security was not enough.

00:25:46.120 --> 00:25:51.530
The US Computer Emergency Response Team
had actually

00:25:51.530 --> 00:25:55.100
identified that there was an exfiltration
from the Office of Personnel Management.

00:25:55.100 --> 00:25:57.930
It was kind of part of breach one.

00:25:57.930 --> 00:26:03.442
They notified the Office of Personnel
Management and basically, a set of teams

00:26:03.442 --> 00:26:08.720
were set up to monitor the first breach,
and then there was a second breach.

00:26:08.720 --> 00:26:13.010
But basically it was a,
even after that first breach occurred,

00:26:13.010 --> 00:26:18.392
it was discovered that there was
malware on a number of key machines,

00:26:18.392 --> 00:26:22.710
including I believe, a jump box.

00:26:22.710 --> 00:26:26.980
A jump box is typically a machine
that's in between ones that are in

00:26:26.980 --> 00:26:30.840
the development and test the staging
network for one's production network.

00:26:30.840 --> 00:26:35.566
And that machine that was infected,
seemed to be

00:26:35.566 --> 00:26:40.650
running a process,
a dynamically linked library.

00:26:40.650 --> 00:26:45.137
That had a particular
name that was actually

00:26:45.137 --> 00:26:49.510
indicative of McAfee Security software.

00:26:51.370 --> 00:26:55.715
Unfortunately, the OPM was
not a McAfee customer.

00:26:55.715 --> 00:26:58.092
That was simply a piece of malware and

00:26:58.092 --> 00:27:01.860
a name being used to masquerade
the attacker's tool kit.

00:27:01.860 --> 00:27:07.480
They were using a root
kit called HighKick.

00:27:07.480 --> 00:27:11.326
And that malware was used to

00:27:11.326 --> 00:27:19.591
reportedly monitor accounts and

00:27:19.591 --> 00:27:23.520
steal more information.

00:27:23.520 --> 00:27:27.854
So, the program there
was then resourced and

00:27:27.854 --> 00:27:31.630
there was a sequence of breaches.

00:27:31.630 --> 00:27:36.280
One of the things that could
have been done to prevent

00:27:36.280 --> 00:27:41.045
this breach was to use multi
factor authentication.

00:27:41.045 --> 00:27:44.846
So that even if say some
machines got infected and

00:27:44.846 --> 00:27:49.310
even if some account credentials
got stolen from there.

00:27:49.310 --> 00:27:51.991
If you require more than
just a username and

00:27:51.991 --> 00:27:56.833
password to access certain critical
systems, then and say there's some two

00:27:56.833 --> 00:28:01.173
factor code that gets sent to
the person's mobile phone or whatnot.

00:28:01.173 --> 00:28:05.432
Then the attacker would've had to infect
not only a bunch of machines, but

00:28:05.432 --> 00:28:06.862
also people's phones.

00:28:06.862 --> 00:28:11.000
So that could've potentially helped.

00:28:11.000 --> 00:28:14.759
One of the challenges that the Office of
Personnel Management had was they had

00:28:14.759 --> 00:28:15.720
a lot of legacy systems.

00:28:16.880 --> 00:28:18.500
And keeping legacy systems patched so

00:28:18.500 --> 00:28:23.000
that they can't be affected by
malware can often be a challenge.

00:28:23.000 --> 00:28:25.720
So that's a little bit about the Office
of Personnel Management breach.

00:28:28.020 --> 00:28:30.900
Going back a year, or
actually also in 2015,

00:28:30.900 --> 00:28:33.250
let me also chat about the Anthem breach.

00:28:34.310 --> 00:28:40.340
Anthem provides healthcare services to
a significant fraction of the country.

00:28:40.340 --> 00:28:46.500
And in 2015 they had a breach where 80
million customer records were stolen.

00:28:46.500 --> 00:28:49.770
Those records included people's names,
their dates of birth,

00:28:49.770 --> 00:28:53.490
their Social Security numbers,
their healthcare ID's.

00:28:53.490 --> 00:28:58.750
And there have been more than
100 lawsuits that occurred.

00:28:58.750 --> 00:29:00.598
In this particular case,

00:29:00.598 --> 00:29:04.889
there was a database user's
credentials that were stolen.

00:29:04.889 --> 00:29:10.588
And the way the breach got noticed
is that one of the administrators

00:29:10.588 --> 00:29:17.429
had noticed that there was a database
inquiry running that they didn't issue.

00:29:17.429 --> 00:29:22.415
And so the issue I think occurred, the
initial compromise might've happened in

00:29:22.415 --> 00:29:27.242
say April, but in December or January,
months later this query was noticed to

00:29:27.242 --> 00:29:30.820
be running and it was taking
up a lot of system resources.

00:29:30.820 --> 00:29:32.930
And so once it was investigated.

00:29:32.930 --> 00:29:37.150
That the credentials were stolen.

00:29:37.150 --> 00:29:41.973
It set off the whole
incident response process.

00:29:41.973 --> 00:29:48.440
I have a next slide here on how the Anthem
breach happened in more detail.

00:29:48.440 --> 00:29:52.500
And we'll return to how it could
have been prevented in just a bit.

00:29:52.500 --> 00:29:57.671
Basically, there were a couple steps,
the attacker sent in a phishing

00:29:57.671 --> 00:30:02.100
email with a link to Wellpoint.com.

00:30:02.100 --> 00:30:05.235
We11point spelled with two
ones instead of two Ls.

00:30:05.235 --> 00:30:10.453
The victim clicked on it.

00:30:10.453 --> 00:30:15.310
The victim machine got
infected just from the click.

00:30:15.310 --> 00:30:18.282
Malware was installed in
the victims system and

00:30:18.282 --> 00:30:21.170
then that system got monitored, right?

00:30:21.170 --> 00:30:25.169
Once the attacker owns the system,
they can monitor all traffic that goes

00:30:25.169 --> 00:30:29.690
through it and they were able to
monitor for account credentials.

00:30:32.300 --> 00:30:38.130
So what happened from there is that
the attacker stole the credentials and

00:30:38.130 --> 00:30:42.990
used those credentials to start posting
queries against Anthem's databases and

00:30:42.990 --> 00:30:46.970
then issued a query for
this 80 million data set.

00:30:48.250 --> 00:30:53.483
And that's the point at which
the attacker got discovered.

00:30:53.483 --> 00:30:55.410
So, that's the Anthem Breach.

00:30:55.410 --> 00:30:57.920
I'll talk about just one or
two more breaches.

00:30:57.920 --> 00:31:02.158
I'll talk about the JPMorgan Chase
breach that happened in 2014.

00:31:02.158 --> 00:31:05.915
In this particular breach, what got
stolen was 70 million customer names and

00:31:05.915 --> 00:31:07.530
email addresses.

00:31:07.530 --> 00:31:10.591
One of the things that was
interesting about this breach,

00:31:10.591 --> 00:31:14.727
if you could imagine that JPMorgan Chase,
as a bank invests a lot in security.

00:31:14.727 --> 00:31:19.419
And this kind of information stolen,
it can be used for

00:31:19.419 --> 00:31:22.850
mass potential phishing attacks.

00:31:22.850 --> 00:31:23.530
Of course,

00:31:23.530 --> 00:31:28.435
because the attacker stole the information
from this dataset at JPMorgan and Chase.

00:31:28.435 --> 00:31:32.839
They know that all the customers
had JPMorgan Chase accounts.

00:31:32.839 --> 00:31:37.005
So there is that potential for
mass phishing attacks, but

00:31:37.005 --> 00:31:41.850
given that it impacted a bank,
one of the silver linings here in this

00:31:41.850 --> 00:31:46.544
particular breach is that one could
imagine that if it was worse.

00:31:46.544 --> 00:31:51.746
The attackers could have just directly
stolen money out of people's accounts, or

00:31:51.746 --> 00:31:58.250
deleted information, or just took direct
financial control, which didn't happen.

00:31:58.250 --> 00:32:00.050
So it's unfortunate that
the attack occurred, but

00:32:00.050 --> 00:32:01.920
it could have been a lot worse.

00:32:01.920 --> 00:32:03.900
How did this particular attack occur?

00:32:03.900 --> 00:32:09.170
Again, there was a phishing email that was
sent to steal some employee credentials.

00:32:09.170 --> 00:32:13.230
Then those employee credentials
were leveraged by the attacker.

00:32:13.230 --> 00:32:18.228
Now JPMorgan Chase did have two factor
authentication deployed on many,

00:32:18.228 --> 00:32:20.330
many, many of their servers.

00:32:20.330 --> 00:32:25.118
Unfortunately, there was one third party
server that was a nonprofit that did

00:32:25.118 --> 00:32:26.910
some work for the bank.

00:32:26.910 --> 00:32:32.010
I believe that they helped
organize fundraisers,

00:32:32.010 --> 00:32:33.470
marathons, something like that.

00:32:33.470 --> 00:32:37.526
But basically there was one server on
which two factor authentication was not

00:32:37.526 --> 00:32:38.170
deployed.

00:32:38.170 --> 00:32:42.012
And so the attacker was able
to use the stolen username and

00:32:42.012 --> 00:32:44.021
password to get to this data.

00:32:44.021 --> 00:32:46.660
So, what could have prevented it?

00:32:46.660 --> 00:32:47.760
Well, anti-phishing training.

00:32:51.570 --> 00:32:56.140
If employees can be better trained
to recognize phishing attacks and or

00:32:56.140 --> 00:32:59.080
more quickly report them,
so much the better.

00:32:59.080 --> 00:33:01.360
And, of course, you've gotta
get two factors on all servers.

00:33:01.360 --> 00:33:04.790
If you miss even one,
attackers can take advantage of that.

00:33:04.790 --> 00:33:06.850
Now the JPMorgan Chase breach.

00:33:06.850 --> 00:33:09.370
And finally,
I'll talk about the Target breach.

00:33:09.370 --> 00:33:14.033
In the Target breach in 2013, what got
stolen was 40 million credit card numbers,

00:33:14.033 --> 00:33:15.280
that's the bad news.

00:33:15.280 --> 00:33:18.659
The good news is that you can reset
people's credit card numbers, and

00:33:18.659 --> 00:33:22.240
customers are typically not
held accountable or liable.

00:33:22.240 --> 00:33:27.560
But the way that this
occurred was actually

00:33:27.560 --> 00:33:34.960
initially via a third-party
supplier that Target used.

00:33:34.960 --> 00:33:39.960
They had a firm by the name of Fazio
Mechanical Services that was responsible

00:33:39.960 --> 00:33:44.470
for running the air conditioning and
heating at all of the retail branches.

00:33:44.470 --> 00:33:48.610
And the attacker first broke into
faulty mechanical services and

00:33:48.610 --> 00:33:52.250
took advantage of the fact that their
networks were connected together.

00:33:52.250 --> 00:33:59.337
And they started sending out a whole
bunch of malware-laden phishing emails,

00:33:59.337 --> 00:34:05.900
and over a series of months, basically
got to a point where they were able to,

00:34:05.900 --> 00:34:11.330
with malware, infect the point of sale
stations that were in the retail stores.

00:34:11.330 --> 00:34:16.152
And they were able to start
exfiltrating data out of those point

00:34:16.152 --> 00:34:20.320
of sale stations as customers
would scan their cards.

00:34:20.320 --> 00:34:25.848
So, in this particular case, network
segmentation could've helped if their air

00:34:25.848 --> 00:34:31.217
conditioning network access was segmented
off from the rest of the company's access,

00:34:31.217 --> 00:34:34.120
that could've helped deterred
the attackers further.

00:34:35.870 --> 00:34:43.121
And Target did have some
anti-malware technology deployed.

00:34:43.121 --> 00:34:47.550
They had a set of folks
outsourced abroad that were

00:34:47.550 --> 00:34:51.440
kind of looking through all the alerts.

00:34:51.440 --> 00:34:54.945
And there were a significant number
of alerts that indicated that there

00:34:54.945 --> 00:34:58.370
was a breach going on, there were
malware infections taking place.

00:34:58.370 --> 00:35:02.690
Some of those notifications were getting
escalated up to their headquarters, but

00:35:02.690 --> 00:35:07.870
there were so
many alerts that they couldn't and

00:35:07.870 --> 00:35:10.000
weren't able to respond to them.

00:35:10.000 --> 00:35:14.610
And there might have been enough false
positive alerts, fake alarms effectively,

00:35:14.610 --> 00:35:18.860
that it may have been hard to discern,
well what were the real alerts?

00:35:18.860 --> 00:35:20.660
Where was the real malicious
activity happening?

00:35:22.030 --> 00:35:24.920
So, in any case, that surveyed a whole
bunch of the big-name breaches that

00:35:24.920 --> 00:35:27.720
have taken place over the past four years.

00:35:27.720 --> 00:35:33.450
I'm going to talk about some of
the key attack vectors and defenses.

00:35:34.920 --> 00:35:40.000
In all of these big name attacks,
phishing and malware have been used as

00:35:40.000 --> 00:35:44.760
tools of choice by the attackers, so I'm
going to talk about a bunch of defenses.

00:35:44.760 --> 00:35:45.610
When it comes to phishing,

00:35:45.610 --> 00:35:51.260
I mentioned that one of the things that
you can do is train your employees,

00:35:51.260 --> 00:35:54.140
give them awareness training, for
how to recognize phishing attacks.

00:35:54.140 --> 00:35:56.280
How do you recognize malicious domains?

00:35:56.280 --> 00:36:01.310
How do you recognize malicious URLs?

00:36:01.310 --> 00:36:03.510
And in many organizations,

00:36:03.510 --> 00:36:08.540
one good practice is to regularly
try to phish your own employees.

00:36:08.540 --> 00:36:13.040
The security team sends out
fake phishing emails and

00:36:14.370 --> 00:36:20.240
sees what percentage of the employees
fall for the test attack.

00:36:20.240 --> 00:36:24.230
And then continue quarter over quarter,

00:36:24.230 --> 00:36:30.266
year over year with helping
the employee base just get better and

00:36:30.266 --> 00:36:34.060
better at recognizing such attacks.

00:36:34.060 --> 00:36:37.640
One of the interesting things that can
happen in those things is that when you

00:36:37.640 --> 00:36:43.560
train employees to recognize attacks and
then have them start sending in attacks,

00:36:43.560 --> 00:36:46.930
what can happen from time to time is
they'll send in an email saying, ha, ha,

00:36:46.930 --> 00:36:51.870
ha, you tried to get me with your
fake attack, but I caught you.

00:36:51.870 --> 00:36:55.426
And sometimes the email response that
comes back from the security team

00:36:55.426 --> 00:36:58.530
is actually, that wasn't us,
that was a real attack.

00:36:58.530 --> 00:37:02.732
So thank you for being a human
sensor to complement all of our

00:37:02.732 --> 00:37:05.970
technology to help us gather data
on when we're getting attacked.

00:37:07.620 --> 00:37:11.440
Part of such training that is
important is to recognize and

00:37:11.440 --> 00:37:16.380
train employees to report phishing attacks
because once phishing attacks do occur,

00:37:16.380 --> 00:37:17.500
it's not necessarily game over.

00:37:18.690 --> 00:37:22.930
If some user did get duped into providing
their user name and password and

00:37:22.930 --> 00:37:27.160
then reports it in, well, security
team can help reset those credentials.

00:37:27.160 --> 00:37:30.680
So that the credentials that
got stolen are unaffected, or

00:37:30.680 --> 00:37:34.230
let's say that somebody's system
got infected with malware.

00:37:34.230 --> 00:37:38.709
Well, you can take steps to contain that
malware and reimage your machine and

00:37:38.709 --> 00:37:39.980
cut off the attacks.

00:37:39.980 --> 00:37:42.040
But there's gotta be that training.

00:37:43.320 --> 00:37:46.470
So the fast reporting of
credentials is important.

00:37:46.470 --> 00:37:50.844
Other technologies that can be important
in making email more secure Is

00:37:50.844 --> 00:37:55.941
leveraging technologies like SPF and
DKIM and DMARC that in SPF,

00:37:55.941 --> 00:37:59.440
you can declare, well, what are the.

00:37:59.440 --> 00:38:01.210
You can imagine that attackers.

00:38:01.210 --> 00:38:05.080
If you're working for
a company called fubar.com,

00:38:05.080 --> 00:38:09.050
attackers might want to spoof
emails coming from that domain so

00:38:09.050 --> 00:38:11.510
that it appears like it's
coming from the organization.

00:38:11.510 --> 00:38:16.690
And by using technologies like SPF and
DKIM and DMARC and by assigning all emails

00:38:16.690 --> 00:38:21.725
that go out from the organization and
instructing the internet

00:38:21.725 --> 00:38:26.607
to drop such emails, you can make it much,
much harder for attackers

00:38:26.607 --> 00:38:30.520
to try to spoof your own domain and
kind of force them to use other domains.

00:38:30.520 --> 00:38:33.645
And so then, if users have
the appropriate training, to look out for

00:38:33.645 --> 00:38:37.160
spammy looking domains, it can help.

00:38:37.160 --> 00:38:41.630
And then another important defense is to
leverage multi-factor authentication so

00:38:41.630 --> 00:38:45.510
that even if attackers steal a particular
username and password credential,

00:38:45.510 --> 00:38:50.540
that credential in and of itself cannot
be used to access a critical system and

00:38:50.540 --> 00:38:54.440
it requires another two factor
code to come via another out

00:38:54.440 --> 00:38:58.520
of band channel in order to
provide access to a system.

00:38:58.520 --> 00:39:02.078
Some phishing and some defenses,
and then there's malware.

00:39:02.078 --> 00:39:06.850
Having important anti-malware
defenses is very important.

00:39:06.850 --> 00:39:11.165
And it's actually important to go beyond
just traditional antivirus which might use

00:39:11.165 --> 00:39:16.060
signature-based techniques to recognize
malware that's been already deployed.

00:39:16.060 --> 00:39:21.520
But you ought to defend against Malware
that hasn't been recognized yet.

00:39:21.520 --> 00:39:26.387
So various input production products
on the market, in addition to using

00:39:26.387 --> 00:39:29.660
signatures, they'll use heuristics,
they'll use machine learning,

00:39:29.660 --> 00:39:34.150
they'll leverage cloud intelligence from
samples that have come in from customers

00:39:35.320 --> 00:39:40.750
to achieve a network effect in detection,
those are all good detectives.

00:39:40.750 --> 00:39:45.540
In addition, when there's any
piece of executable code that

00:39:45.540 --> 00:39:50.600
looks like it might be suspicious,
one can leverage what's called

00:39:50.600 --> 00:39:54.918
sandbox based detection where that malware
gets spun up into a virtual machine.

00:39:54.918 --> 00:39:59.950
And you test,
well what does that binary do?

00:39:59.950 --> 00:40:04.790
So even if there's no signature for it,
maybe if it sets off some heuristics,

00:40:04.790 --> 00:40:08.600
you say well, what does it actually do
when it starts running on a machine?

00:40:09.980 --> 00:40:13.240
There's also intrusion detection
technology that can be used,

00:40:13.240 --> 00:40:16.732
such as Norton Pro, that are well
known intrusion detection systems,

00:40:16.732 --> 00:40:19.193
which do take advantage of signatures, but

00:40:19.193 --> 00:40:23.310
basically gives you detection
not just on the host themselves,

00:40:23.310 --> 00:40:26.830
but at the network layer, at the firewall
layer and other parts of the network.

00:40:26.830 --> 00:40:32.490
And then one other thing that you can do
is on machines that could potentially get

00:40:32.490 --> 00:40:38.500
infected, limit the privileges that users
are allowed to have on their machines.

00:40:38.500 --> 00:40:43.911
You can imagine that if you can limit
privileges and say not let users install

00:40:43.911 --> 00:40:49.048
software on their machines, then If even
some attacker compromises that machine,

00:40:49.048 --> 00:40:51.270
they won't be able to
install malicious software.

00:40:51.270 --> 00:40:55.460
So limiting the privileges
can be important.

00:40:55.460 --> 00:40:58.300
I talked about the attack against Yahoo.

00:40:58.300 --> 00:41:02.831
So it is important, if you run web
applications to make sure that the cookies

00:41:02.831 --> 00:41:05.110
that your site generates are strong.

00:41:06.470 --> 00:41:09.080
And very often what goes into cookies,

00:41:10.130 --> 00:41:14.230
they get generated based
off of some secret key or

00:41:14.230 --> 00:41:18.714
some combination of a secret key
plus a per user [INAUDIBLE].

00:41:18.714 --> 00:41:23.740
So it's important to protect those keys,
have strong key management.

00:41:23.740 --> 00:41:30.243
And all these topics are topics covered in
a number of our courses here at Stanford.

00:41:30.243 --> 00:41:37.530
Let me chat a little bit about
some sensors more generally.

00:41:37.530 --> 00:41:40.780
I've talked a lot about
the big name breaches here.

00:41:40.780 --> 00:41:45.282
Phishing and malware have been a set
of tools that attackers have used to

00:41:45.282 --> 00:41:47.010
accomplish these big name breaches.

00:41:47.010 --> 00:41:51.676
But you know,
most breaches in fact because of mistakes

00:41:51.676 --> 00:41:55.760
made by employees or some set of humans.

00:41:55.760 --> 00:42:00.163
And so it's important to not
just have anti malware and

00:42:00.163 --> 00:42:04.757
anti-phising defenses, but
to have a set of defenses,

00:42:04.757 --> 00:42:10.234
that when employees at an organization
to have a holistic defense.

00:42:10.234 --> 00:42:14.361
And to have countermeasures that
defend not only against the most

00:42:14.361 --> 00:42:19.430
sophisticated cyber criminals,
these kind of big name types of attacks.

00:42:19.430 --> 00:42:23.070
But also defend against all
the inadvertent things that can go wrong.

00:42:23.070 --> 00:42:28.053
And so employing an approach where you
have some countermeasures that prevent

00:42:28.053 --> 00:42:29.267
,is a good thing.

00:42:29.267 --> 00:42:34.160
For instance, data minimization, if you
don't need the data, don't store it.

00:42:34.160 --> 00:42:36.700
If you don't store it, it can't be stolen.

00:42:36.700 --> 00:42:41.000
There is a whole bunch of other
prevention approaches that's here.

00:42:41.000 --> 00:42:44.264
But you got a couple back
together with good detection,

00:42:44.264 --> 00:42:47.710
good containment and
good recovery counter measures.

00:42:47.710 --> 00:42:53.069
How one reacts when there's issues can be
just as important as preventing issues

00:42:53.069 --> 00:42:58.200
in and of themselves, is one of the things
that's been learned over the years.

00:42:58.200 --> 00:43:04.282
If you're an organization and you're
working to improve your security and

00:43:04.282 --> 00:43:06.790
monitor your security,
how can you do that?

00:43:06.790 --> 00:43:08.450
And how can you do that quantitatively?

00:43:10.080 --> 00:43:14.458
So, there's a number of services
these days that allow you to measure

00:43:14.458 --> 00:43:15.860
your security posture.

00:43:15.860 --> 00:43:20.290
And so the SecurityScorecard is a company
that's funded by Google Ventures.

00:43:20.290 --> 00:43:24.019
They provide one such service where
they measure ten different areas of your

00:43:24.019 --> 00:43:25.230
security.

00:43:25.230 --> 00:43:28.510
And for instance they measure
your end point security,

00:43:28.510 --> 00:43:33.250
they help identify if you do have infected
machines on your network that might

00:43:33.250 --> 00:43:37.370
be participating in bot nets,
networks of compromised machines.

00:43:37.370 --> 00:43:42.000
And they do that by monitoring
the bot networks and

00:43:42.000 --> 00:43:44.100
what IP they're connecting in them.

00:43:44.100 --> 00:43:47.740
And are any of those IP addresses yours?

00:43:47.740 --> 00:43:50.670
But employer service like this
can help you get a sense for

00:43:50.670 --> 00:43:54.464
your overall security posture and

00:43:54.464 --> 00:43:59.679
can be very good in helping you figure
out where you need to make improvements.

00:43:59.679 --> 00:44:01.436
One of the challenges, of course,

00:44:01.436 --> 00:44:04.110
is that such things may not
be fully comprehensive.

00:44:04.110 --> 00:44:09.261
For instance are you using
two factor authentication?

00:44:09.261 --> 00:44:13.040
Are you doing phishing tests and
anti-phishing training?

00:44:13.040 --> 00:44:17.200
That may or may not be directly
captured by some of these

00:44:18.280 --> 00:44:20.180
measurements in a number of these areas.

00:44:20.180 --> 00:44:23.515
A typical security score card
though will have a lot of data.

00:44:23.515 --> 00:44:27.480
It'll have some overview that gives you
grades in these different areas and

00:44:27.480 --> 00:44:29.790
helps you monitor the data over time.

00:44:29.790 --> 00:44:34.300
This is a sample report for a fictional
organization called Evergrocery,

00:44:35.390 --> 00:44:37.430
but can be helpful.

00:44:37.430 --> 00:44:40.040
For instance, looking at patching seasons.

00:44:40.040 --> 00:44:45.335
If you have any machines that are not
being patched regularly externally,

00:44:45.335 --> 00:44:48.350
then it could be susceptible
to incidents like WannaCry.

00:44:48.350 --> 00:44:51.040
So this kind of data can be helpful.

00:44:52.500 --> 00:44:57.167
Another such tool that could
be used is a tool called

00:44:57.167 --> 00:45:01.225
QuadMetrics that was
acquired actually by FICO,

00:45:01.225 --> 00:45:05.160
the same organization that
monitors credit scores.

00:45:05.160 --> 00:45:09.550
And the reason I mention QuadMetrics
is because they are also looking at

00:45:09.550 --> 00:45:12.380
what are many of
the different features and

00:45:12.380 --> 00:45:16.740
aspects that one can observe to
assess external security posture.

00:45:16.740 --> 00:45:21.710
And I mentioned them because they have
some published researched back in 2015,

00:45:21.710 --> 00:45:26.173
a number of the early employees and
the founders I believe of

00:45:26.173 --> 00:45:30.900
the company published a paper at
the [INAUDIBLE] security symposium.

00:45:30.900 --> 00:45:35.588
And in their paper they
described how they looked at 258

00:45:35.588 --> 00:45:40.110
features that one can just observe and

00:45:40.110 --> 00:45:44.570
see if there's any mismanagement happening
within an organization's security.

00:45:44.570 --> 00:45:47.800
Are there SSL certificates
that are misconfigured?

00:45:47.800 --> 00:45:52.760
Are some of the IPs that
an organization owns involved in doing.

00:45:52.760 --> 00:45:55.195
They're having phishing attacks and

00:45:55.195 --> 00:45:59.681
they built a random forest predictive
classifier, so that they could

00:45:59.681 --> 00:46:03.725
predict which organizations
are most likely to get breached.

00:46:03.725 --> 00:46:08.673
Without getting into the details we cover
this in more detail in our cyber security

00:46:08.673 --> 00:46:11.010
for executives course.

00:46:11.010 --> 00:46:15.216
They trained some of their models and
then looked at well back in 2014,

00:46:15.216 --> 00:46:19.173
could they predict which organizations
might've gotten breached?

00:46:19.173 --> 00:46:22.050
And based on the way the classifier was
trained, it came up with pretty high

00:46:22.050 --> 00:46:24.670
scores for some of the organizations
that were breached.

00:46:24.670 --> 00:46:27.810
Now of course the real question
is if we use this model today.

00:46:27.810 --> 00:46:31.070
Who they predict is going to get breached
next year and the year after that.

00:46:31.070 --> 00:46:34.930
But it's really good to see the field
getting more quantitative and

00:46:34.930 --> 00:46:39.210
bringing in more data to help
assess security posture and

00:46:39.210 --> 00:46:41.670
help organizations improve their security.

00:46:41.670 --> 00:46:45.190
So with that, I'll just provide a couple
of pointers where you can learn more.

00:46:45.190 --> 00:46:48.021
I mentioned the Cyber Security and

00:46:48.021 --> 00:46:52.320
Executive Strategy course
that we just rolled out.

00:46:52.320 --> 00:46:57.090
That helps you as a manager or

00:46:57.090 --> 00:47:00.780
as an executive understand,
how do you measure security?

00:47:00.780 --> 00:47:02.490
How do you report to the board?

00:47:02.490 --> 00:47:03.870
How you report to other management.

00:47:05.010 --> 00:47:06.660
We have a course on emerging
threats on defenses.

00:47:06.660 --> 00:47:09.720
We also have a course on
software security foundations.

00:47:09.720 --> 00:47:13.740
So if you are just getting into
the field and want to learn more,

00:47:13.740 --> 00:47:15.740
that's a great course to take.

00:47:15.740 --> 00:47:20.034
There's a bunch of other links here to
some resources on the various breaches

00:47:20.034 --> 00:47:21.233
that I talked about.

00:47:21.233 --> 00:47:25.500
For instance, a link to the indictment
of the Russian spy with cyber criminals.

00:47:25.500 --> 00:47:29.669
In the case of the OPM breach there's a
link here to this house oversight majority

00:47:29.669 --> 00:47:30.480
staff report.

00:47:30.480 --> 00:47:35.203
There's a link to a presentation by
some folks from Anthem themselves about

00:47:35.203 --> 00:47:37.208
that how that attack occurred.

00:47:37.208 --> 00:47:41.900
And with that I hope you have
enjoyed learning how these big

00:47:41.900 --> 00:47:44.399
name breaches have occurred.

00:47:45.440 --> 00:47:48.134
And hope that you've enjoyed learning
a little bit about some of the counter

00:47:48.134 --> 00:47:50.792
measures that could help in
how the breaches occurred.

00:47:50.792 --> 00:47:55.530
So that hopefully we can all
learn from these lessons and

00:47:55.530 --> 00:48:00.800
prevent some of these kinds of
breaches from occurring in the future.

00:48:00.800 --> 00:48:01.397
Thank you.

00:48:01.397 --> 00:48:06.475
&gt;&gt; All right, so now we'll take a few
minutes to answer some of your questions.

00:48:06.475 --> 00:48:09.941
Looks like the first one we have is,
with breaches being a part

00:48:09.941 --> 00:48:14.008
of the mainstream media in the past
few years, particularly politics,

00:48:14.008 --> 00:48:18.390
security has now become a much
higher priority for my company.

00:48:18.390 --> 00:48:21.990
We have recently been approved for
a significant budget increase next year.

00:48:21.990 --> 00:48:25.120
And are discussing proposed strategies.

00:48:25.120 --> 00:48:30.970
What areas do you think will
provide the best investment?

00:48:30.970 --> 00:48:37.470
&gt;&gt; So, that is a question that will
depend upon your organization and

00:48:37.470 --> 00:48:44.390
what, who's targeting you,
what's your attack surface.

00:48:44.390 --> 00:48:50.501
And so, to figure out what might
be some of the best areas for

00:48:50.501 --> 00:48:55.267
investment, what I suggest is,
as step one,

00:48:55.267 --> 00:49:02.357
to do a risk assessment, and
have interviews with their employees,

00:49:02.357 --> 00:49:06.393
have bringing a lot of quantitative and

00:49:06.393 --> 00:49:11.740
qualitative data and
access what the risks are.

00:49:11.740 --> 00:49:16.497
And then, figure out what are the counter
measures, whether it be people, profit,

00:49:16.497 --> 00:49:19.590
technology that what's put in place,
to address those risks.

00:49:19.590 --> 00:49:22.980
That's the first question,

00:49:22.980 --> 00:49:27.460
let me take a stab at the next question.

00:49:27.460 --> 00:49:31.250
Next question here is,
can we define breach?

00:49:31.250 --> 00:49:33.734
I've heard this word used
in different places, but

00:49:33.734 --> 00:49:35.328
I don't know the exact meaning?

00:49:35.328 --> 00:49:39.417
For example,
is breach mean data is compromised, or

00:49:39.417 --> 00:49:43.980
when it only refers to
certain sets of standards?

00:49:43.980 --> 00:49:44.860
And so,

00:49:44.860 --> 00:49:48.820
the question of what is the definition
of breach is an interesting one.

00:49:48.820 --> 00:49:52.680
I would actually encourage
you to consult an attorney.

00:49:52.680 --> 00:49:59.238
There are data breach laws in 45,
47 plus of the states that have them and

00:49:59.238 --> 00:50:04.280
they all define breach
a little bit differently.

00:50:04.280 --> 00:50:05.836
The definition, again,

00:50:05.836 --> 00:50:10.658
of California was the first state that
had such a database fallback in 2003.

00:50:10.658 --> 00:50:15.440
But the law is generally around the form
that if there is unencrypted data that's

00:50:15.440 --> 00:50:20.360
stolen that contains, say, somebody's
name, or certain type of identifiers,

00:50:20.360 --> 00:50:22.704
whatever, then it counts as a breach.

00:50:22.704 --> 00:50:26.390
And each state's definitions
are a little bit different.

00:50:26.390 --> 00:50:29.380
And there's that legal
definition of a breach.

00:50:29.380 --> 00:50:34.150
Sometimes, I've seen technical
folks throw around the word breach.

00:50:34.150 --> 00:50:39.553
But first it's,
let's say that infect some system.

00:50:39.553 --> 00:50:43.985
Well, if there's no customer or
personal identifiable information,

00:50:43.985 --> 00:50:45.630
that may not be considered a breach.

00:50:46.880 --> 00:50:50.270
So, the answer is consult an attorney.

00:50:50.270 --> 00:50:55.510
An attorney will help you understand
what's the legal definition of breach,

00:50:55.510 --> 00:50:57.190
and go from there.

00:50:57.190 --> 00:51:00.940
I think we have time for
maybe one more question.

00:51:03.200 --> 00:51:07.820
And let's see,
I can only choose, I guess, one.

00:51:10.870 --> 00:51:15.840
Let's see, there's a question here of,
I have a question about the attack on.

00:51:15.840 --> 00:51:20.695
How long could this hacker maintain
connectivity with the victim in terms

00:51:20.695 --> 00:51:21.910
of duration?

00:51:21.910 --> 00:51:25.898
The answer to that is that
an attacker could, in theory,

00:51:25.898 --> 00:51:31.040
maintain connectivity indefinitely until

00:51:31.040 --> 00:51:36.947
the connectivity is cut off for
some reason.

00:51:36.947 --> 00:51:41.320
So, how long an attacker wants
to maintain connectivity,

00:51:42.340 --> 00:51:48.080
how fast can an organization identify it
and cut it off, those are all variables.

00:51:48.080 --> 00:51:50.586
But there is nothing
inherent about the attack on

00:51:51.720 --> 00:51:55.850
that would limit how long an attacker
can maintain connectivity.

00:51:55.850 --> 00:52:02.310
And so, with attacks having
become more mission-based,

00:52:02.310 --> 00:52:07.867
the attackers have been
maintaining connectivity

00:52:07.867 --> 00:52:12.140
much longer than they have in the past.

00:52:12.140 --> 00:52:13.830
It can range on the order of months or
years.

00:52:16.632 --> 00:52:18.740
&gt;&gt; Well, great, thank you so much, Neil.

00:52:18.740 --> 00:52:22.030
Looks like that's all the time
we have left for this webinar.

00:52:22.030 --> 00:52:24.866
Thank you all for joining us, and
we hope you have a great rest of your day.

